Wait! I see it now. The `max_len` is computed once at the start. But `ssh_buffer_get_u32(buffer, &u32len)` reads 4 bytes from the buffer, advancing `buffer->pos`. After this, the remaining data is `max_len - 4`. But the check `len > max_len - 1` uses the original `max_len`, not `max_len - 4`.

So if `max_len = 4` (buffer has exactly 4 bytes), after reading the 4-byte length, the remaining data is 0. But `max_len - 1 = 3`. So `len` can be up to 3. Then `ssh_buffer_validate_length(buffer, 3)` checks `buffer->pos + 3 > buffer->used`, i.e., `4 + 3 > 4`, which is true, so it fails. OK, safe.

But what if `max_len = 5`? After reading 4 bytes, remaining is 1. `max_len - 1 = 4`. So `len` can be up to 4. `ssh_buffer_validate_length(buffer, 4)` checks `4 + 4 > 5`, which is true, so it fails. `len = 1` passes: `4 + 1 > 5` is false. OK, correct.

So the stale `max_len` doesn't cause an issue because `ssh_buffer_validate_length` is the real check.

